# Promote → tag / release (required)

`staging` promotes to `main` only when Patrick says **ship** or **promote to prod**. Agents do not merge that pull request, tag, or publish a GitHub Release before that. The release pull request is `staging` → `main`. See `RELEASING.md`.

When an agent **merges or completes that promote**, they **MUST** publish the SemVer release on `main` in the same turn — do **not** wait for a separate “tag it” request. `.github/workflows/release-publish.yml` runs the same command on the push to `main`. It is idempotent: a second run does not create another tag.

```bash
git checkout main && git pull origin main
npm run release:publish -- --confirm
```

That runs release-gate → annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` → push tag → `gh release create` (notes from `CHANGELOG.md`). In Actions, tag push and release create use `SYNC_BOT_TOKEN` when that secret is set, so `close-shipped-issues` can start. Do not invent or print the token.

## Guards

- Tag **only** on `main` after the promote merge lands (never mid-train, never on feature/`staging`-only tips).
- If the GitHub Release for this version already exists, stop. The publish command exits 0. Do not force a second tag.
- Skip a Dependabot-only promote with `skip-version-bump` and no version change: do not invent a tag. The publish command no-ops when that version is already released.
- Never retag or move an existing `vX.Y.Z`; ship a new version instead.
- After publish, `release-sync-staging` opens `release-sync/vX.Y.Z` into `staging` when `SYNC_BOT_TOKEN` is set. That sync is not a second promote.

## Closing issues

GitHub applies `Closes` / `Fixes` / `Resolves` when a pull request merges into the default branch (`main`). Feature PRs merge into `staging`, so those keywords stay open until the release that contains them is published.

- Use `Closes #N` on a feature PR only when that PR fully finishes the issue.
- Use `Part of #N` when the PR is a slice of a larger issue or epic. `Related #N` and `Slice A of #N` stay open too. A closing keyword is the only phrase that closes.
- Label an issue `keep-open` when it must stay open even if a shipped PR uses a closing keyword (epics and partial ships).
- `.github/workflows/close-shipped-issues.yml` runs on `release: published`. It comments `Shipped in <tag> (#<promote>) via #<source PR>` and closes the issue with `state_reason: completed`. Already-closed issues, pull request numbers, and `keep-open` issues are skipped. A second run for the same tag does not comment again and does not reopen anything.
- Dry run: Actions → **Close shipped issues** → Run workflow. Set `tag` (for example `v1.96.0`) and leave `dry_run` checked. The job logs the would-close list and comments it on the promote PR. Uncheck `dry_run` to close for real. The workflow file is read from `main`, so the dispatch exists only after this change is promoted.
- Pre-release tags (`vX.Y.Z-rc.N`) do not close issues.

